<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="palmitoleate biosynthesis II (plants and bacteria)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-9245 Palmitoleate| (16:1&amp;Delta;9) is a common unsaturated fatty acid, found in bacteria, fungi |CITS: [8294460]|, plants |CITS: [7548115]| and animals |CITS:[14321753] [6030882]|. It comprises from 10% to 65% of the seed oil of several plants. |FRAME: CPD-9245 Palmitoleate| can be formed directly from the saturated fatty acid |FRAME: PALMITATE palmitate| by the action of &amp;Delta;9 desaturase enzymes. Such enzymes have been reported from cyanobacteria |CITS: [7929259][10937442][16689682][17385315]|, fungi |CITS: [2674136][12055292]|, higher plants |CITS: [12208502][8861937]|, fish |CITS: [11170264]| and mammals |CITS: [16443825]|. Desaturase enzyme do not operate on a free acid substrate. Three types of desaturases are known, that act on acyl-CoA, acyl-[acp], and acyl-lipid conjugates, respectively. In many bacteria and some plants |FRAME: CPD-9245 palmitoleate| is derived from |FRAME: Palmitoyl-ACPs a palmitoyl-[acp]|, as shown in this pathway |CITS: [9154992]|. In mammals the enzyme acts only on CoA-derivatives |CITS: [16443825]|, while in some plants and cyanobacteria the desaturase acts on fatty acids embedded within the structure of lipids |CITS: [7929259]| (see |FRAME: PWY-7589 palmitoleate biosynthesis III (cyanobacteria)|). For an alternative pathway for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-9245 palmitoleate|, found in the bacterium |FRAME:TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;|, see |FRAME: PWY-6282 palmitoleate biosynthesis I (from (5Z)-dodec-5-enoate)|.</Description>
